<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Anthem · 1904 · South &amp; Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="sg-title">Sare Jahan se Accha</h1><p class="sg-local"><bdi lang="ur" dir="rtl">سارے جہاں سے اچھا</bdi> / <bdi lang="ur-Latn">Sāre Jahāṉ se Acchā</bdi></p><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A poem of 1904 by Muhammad Iqbal in praise of the land of Hindustan, sung in Indian schools and by army bands.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Muhammad Iqbal</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>not known (the earlier, slower tune); Ravi Shankar (the tune sung today, 1945)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>the peoples of British India; today sung in India</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Urdu</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Lahore, British India (present-day Pakistan)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>published 16 August 1904</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Anthem</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Muhammad Iqbal (published 1904; he died in 1938))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sare-jahan-se-accha">Sare Jahan se Accha</a>&quot;, formally &quot;Tarānah-e-Hindi&quot; (&quot;Anthem of the People of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindustan">Hindustan</a>&quot;), is an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a> patriotic song for children written by the poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-iqbal">Muhammad Iqbal</a> in the ghazal style of Urdu poetry. It was published in the weekly journal Ittehad on 16 August 1904. Iqbal was then a lecturer at Government College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>, and the next year a student, Lala Har Dayal, invited him to preside over a function there; instead of giving a speech, Iqbal sang the poem. It is an ode to Hindustan, the land that today comprises Bangladesh, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, and it carries a yearning for and attachment to that land, a sense of cultural memory and an elegiac quality. It quickly became an anthem of opposition to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-raj">British Raj</a>, and in 1924 it was published in Iqbal's Urdu book Bang-i-Dara.</p><p>In 1905 the 27-year-old Iqbal saw the future society of the subcontinent as pluralistic, a composite Hindu-Muslim culture. Later that year he left for three years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, which turned him into an Islamic philosopher. In 1910 he wrote another song for children, &quot;Tarana-e-Milli&quot; (&quot;Anthem of the Religious Community&quot;), in the same metre and rhyme scheme, which renounced much of the sentiment of the earlier song: its homeland was no longer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindustan">Hindustan</a> but the whole world. In 1930, in his presidential address to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/all-india-muslim-league">Muslim League</a>'s annual conference at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prayagraj">Allahabad</a>, he supported a separate nation-state in the Muslim-majority areas of the subcontinent, an idea that inspired the creation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>.</p><p>The song has remained popular in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a> is said to have sung it over a hundred times while imprisoned at Yerawada Jail in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Pune</a> in the 1930s. In the 1930s and 1940s it was sung to a slower tune. In 1945, while working in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Mumbai</a> with the Indian People's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">Theatre</a> Association and composing for K. A. Abbas's film Dharti Ke Lal and Chetan Anand's Neecha Nagar, the sitarist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ravi-shankar">Ravi Shankar</a> was asked to set the song; he found the existing tune too slow and sad and gave it a stronger one, which is the tune sung today. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lata-mangeshkar">Lata Mangeshkar</a> later recorded it to a third, quite different tune.</p><p>Four of its stanzas became an unofficial national song in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ravi-shankar">Ravi Shankar</a>'s version, arranged as a march by Antsher Lobo, was adopted as the official quick march of the Indian Armed Forces. The massed bands of the armed forces play it each year on Independence Day, on Republic Day and at the close of Beating Retreat, and schools sing it at morning assemblies. In 1984 Rakesh Sharma, the first Indian astronaut, used its opening line to describe to Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indira-gandhi">Indira Gandhi</a> how India looked from space, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manmohan-singh">Manmohan Singh</a> quoted the poem at his first press conference as prime minister.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>An ode to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindustan">Hindustan</a> as the best of all lands, praising its mountains and rivers and its people's love for it wherever they are. It calls its people, of every faith, one people of one homeland, and reflects on how they have endured through the centuries. The mood is proud and affectionate, with a note of sorrow at its close.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Urdu</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="rom">Romanized</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-3"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Urdu</span><span class="sg-r">Romanized</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ur" dir="rtl">سارے جہاں سے اچھا ہندوستاں ہمارا</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ur-Latn">sāre jahāṉ se achchhā Hindūstāṉ hamārā</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Better than all the world is our Hindustan;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ur" dir="rtl">ہم بلبلیں ہیں اس کی، یہ گلستاں ہمارا</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ur-Latn">ham bulbuleṉ haiṉ is kī, yih gulistāṉ hamārā</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>we are its nightingales, it is our garden.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ur" dir="rtl">غربت میں ہوں اگر ہم، رہتا ہے دل وطن میں</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ur-Latn">ghurbat meṉ hoṉ agar ham, rahtā hai dil vatan meṉ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>If we are in a foreign land, the heart stays in the homeland:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ur" dir="rtl">سمجھو وہیں ہمیں بھی دل ہو جہاں ہمارا</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ur-Latn">samjho vahīṉ hameṉ bhī dil ho jahāṉ hamārā</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>count us, too, to be there, where our heart is.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ur" dir="rtl">پربت وہ سب سے اونچا، ہمسایہ آسماں کا</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ur-Latn">parbat vuh sab se ūṉchā, hamsāyah āsmāṉ kā</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>That mountain, highest of all, neighbour of the sky,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ur" dir="rtl">وہ سنتری ہمارا، وہ پاسباں ہمارا</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ur-Latn">vuh santarī hamārā, vuh pāsbāṉ hamārā</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>it is our sentry, it is our guardian.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ur" dir="rtl">گودی میں کھیلتی ہیں اس کی ہزاروں ندیاں</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ur-Latn">godī meṉ kheltī haiṉ us kī hazāroṉ nadiyāṉ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>In its lap play thousands of rivers,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ur" dir="rtl">گلشن ہے جن کے دم سے رشکِ جناں ہمارا</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ur-Latn">gulshan hai jin ke dam se rashk-i jināṉ hamārā</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>by whose life our garden is the envy of paradise.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ur" dir="rtl">اے آبِ رودِ گنگا! وہ دن ہیں یاد تجھ کو؟</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ur-Latn">ai āb-i rūd-i Gangā! vuh din haiṉ yād tujh ko?</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>O water of the river Ganga! Do you remember those days</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ur" dir="rtl">اترا ترے کنارے جب کارواں ہمارا</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ur-Latn">utrā tire kināre jab kārvāṉ hamārā</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>when our caravan came down to your bank?</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ur" dir="rtl">مذہب نہیں سکھاتا آپس میں بیر رکھنا</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ur-Latn">mazhab nahīṉ sikhātā āpas meṉ bair rakhnā</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Religion does not teach us to keep enmity among ourselves;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ur" dir="rtl">ہندی ہیں ہم، وطن ہے ہندوستاں ہمارا</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ur-Latn">Hindī haiṉ ham, vatan hai Hindūstāṉ hamārā</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>we are of Hind, our homeland is Hindustan.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ur" dir="rtl">یونان و مصر و روما سب مٹ گئے جہاں سے</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ur-Latn">Yūnān o Misr o Rūmā sab miṭ gaʾe jahāṉ se</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Greece and Egypt and Rome have all vanished from the world,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ur" dir="rtl">اب تک مگر ہے باقی نام و نشاں ہمارا</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ur-Latn">ab tak magar hai bāqī nām o nishāṉ hamārā</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>but to this day our name and trace remain.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ur" dir="rtl">کچھ بات ہے کہ ہستی مٹتی نہیں ہماری</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ur-Latn">kuchh bāt hai kih hastī miṭtī nahīṉ hamārī</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>There is something in it, that our being is not wiped out,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ur" dir="rtl">صدیوں رہا ہے دشمن دورِ زماں ہمارا</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ur-Latn">sadiyoṉ rahā hai dushman daur-i zamāṉ hamārā</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>though for centuries the turning of time has been our enemy.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ur" dir="rtl">اقبال! کوئی محرم اپنا نہيں جہاں میں</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ur-Latn">Iqbāl! koʾī mahram apnā nahīṉ jahāṉ meṉ</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Iqbal! We have no confidant in the world:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="ur" dir="rtl">معلوم کیا کسی کو دردِ نہاں ہمارا!</span><span class="sg-r" lang="ur-Latn">maʿlūm kyā kisī ko dard-i nihāṉ hamārā!</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>what does anyone know of our hidden pain!</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Muhammad Iqbal (1904); the Urdu text as quoted in Wikipedia's article. Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sare_Jahan_se_Accha" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p><p>The romanization is the Institute&#8217;s own (ALA-LC romanization of Urdu (without dots under the Arabic letters; ṭ, ḍ, ṛ for the retroflex consonants, ṉ for the nasal)).</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (7)</summary><ul><li>line 2.1: 'ghurbat', being away from home, exile (also poverty).</li><li>line 3.1: the mountain is the Himalaya, unnamed in the poem.</li><li>line 4.2: 'jinān', the gardens of paradise; 'dam', breath or life: the rivers give the garden its life.</li><li>line 5.2: the caravan coming down to the Ganges is the arrival of the poet's forebears (or of a people) in India; the sense is not spelled out.</li><li>line 6.2: 'Hindī' here means a person of Hind, India, not the language.</li><li>line 8.1: literally 'there is some matter (reason) that our existence does not perish'.</li><li>line 9.1: Iqbal names himself, as Urdu poets do in the last couplet; 'mahram', one who shares one's secrets.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
